# Frontend React

Apply when editing frontend React (`.tsx` / `.jsx`) files. Prefer existing patterns in `frontend/` over inventing new structure or state/networking stacks.

## Structure

```text
frontend/src/
├── components/     # reusable UI
├── pages/          # route-level screens
├── hooks/          # reusable stateful logic
├── services/       # API / integration layer
├── utils/          # pure helpers
├── types/          # shared contracts
└── ...
```

## Layering

- Components: presentation; accept props/callbacks; no direct API calls.
- Pages: compose components; wire route params and page-level state.
- Hooks: reusable client state and side-effect behavior.
- Services: HTTP and external integrations only.

## Conventions

- Prefer function components; extract custom hooks for reusable stateful logic.
- Reuse existing components before creating new ones.
- Avoid large components; split when a piece has a clear single responsibility.
- Keep API calls in `services/` (or established data hooks that call services)—not in presentational components.
- Keep business rules that must be trusted on the server.
- Handle loading, success, empty, and error states where relevant.
- Do not introduce a new state-management or data-fetching library without justification.
- Follow the repo's React patterns (e.g. Compiler guidance); do not add `useMemo`/`useCallback` by default unless already used.

## Example

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — presentational component fetches
function UserCard() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/user').then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);
  }, []);
  return <div>{user?.name}</div>;
}

// ✅ GOOD — service + page/hook owns data; component renders
function UserCard({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
}
```
